  "descriptions": {
    "preferred": "An intriguing dwarf laceleaf that reaches 3 ft. (1 m) tall, 'Fairy Hair', as the name implies, develops very thin, threadlike leaves that tend to be bunched together. It does not develop the broader palmate leaves characteristic of the more vigorous 'Koto-no-ito'. The spring leaves are an attractive orange-red, becoming green for the summer. 'Fall's Fire' Matsumurae Group—green. This aptly named cultivar is noted for its spectacular display of yellows, oranges, reds, and purples in the fall. The medium-sized seven-lobed palmate leaves are an Acer attractive yellow-green in the spring. The plant is vigorous and soon forms an upright medium-"
  },
